// The `audio-alsa` loophole, shipped by the OFFICIAL `audio` pack — the dogfood
// for the 15th contribution kind (docs/design/loophole-packaging.md §7, OQ-LP11).
//
// READ THE PACK'S README BEFORE CHANGING ANYTHING HERE. Two things about this
// manifest are surprising and both are forced by measurement rather than taste:
//
//  1. Its NAME is `audio-alsa`, not `audio`. `audio` is a RESERVED loophole name
//     (the bundled loophole owns it), and the launch pre-flight
//     run.PackLoopholeNameConflicts refuses a pack claiming a reserved name —
//     fatally, because the loser's manifest would still contribute its binds and
//     devices while yolo's own record answered to the name.
//  2. It ships the ALSA half of audio and NOT the sockets. The pack-shipped subset
//     (internal/loopholedecl/packshipped.go) refuses a `host_bind_mounts[].host`
//     that expands an environment variable, and every socket a real audio loophole
//     needs is under `${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR}`. There is no home-relative spelling of
//     /run/user/<uid>/pulse, so the socket half is UNEXPRESSIBLE for a pack. That
//     is a finding about the subset, recorded in the README, not a gap to work
//     around here.
//
// The env half (PULSE_SERVER / PIPEWIRE_REMOTE) is NOT here either: `jail_env` is
// refused for a pack-shipped loophole, so the pack declares those through the `env`
// contribution kind, which makes them UNCONDITIONAL. That is §3.1's named honest
// cost and OQ-LP5's trigger; the README says what it costs.
{
  "name": "audio-alsa",
  "description": "ALSA→PipeWire routing for the jail: drops an /etc/alsa/conf.d fragment that points libasound's \"default\" PCM/CTL at the PipeWire shim, so a libasound client reaches the audio the bundled `audio` loophole bridged instead of dying on a missing card.",
  "version": 1,
  "enabled": true,

  // Linux-only, DECLARED rather than left to a `requires` probe (§3.1). The two
  // fields answer different questions: `requires` is "is the thing I need present"
  // (fix: install it), `platforms` is "does this exist for this machine at all"
  // (no fix). ALSA is a Linux kernel interface, so on darwin there is nothing to
  // install — and saying so is what makes the inert report actionable instead of
  // sending a macOS reader after a package that cannot help.
  "platforms": [
    "linux"
  ],

  // No daemon, no interception: this loophole crosses the boundary with one
  // read-only bind of a file the pack itself ships.
  "transport": "none",
  "lifecycle": "external",

  "host_bind_mounts": [
    {
      // {loophole_dir} — the one bind host a pack-shipped loophole may name, and
      // the reason it is allowed: this is content the pack ships, which packstage
      // already vetted for exec bits and escaping symlinks.
      "host": "{loophole_dir}/asound.conf",
      // NOT /etc/asound.conf, and this is the load-bearing half of the whole
      // design. The bundled `audio` loophole binds ITS copy at /etc/asound.conf,
      // and podman refuses two binds on one destination whose sources differ
      // ("duplicate mount destination" — measured), which would make a jail that
      // has both REFUSE TO START. alsa-lib reads /etc/alsa/conf.d BEFORE
      // /etc/asound.conf (measured in alsa.conf's own include list), so a
      // conf.d fragment delivers the same routing with no destination a
      // bundled loophole claims.
      "container": "/etc/alsa/conf.d/50-yolo-audio-alsa.conf",
      // Omitted-defaults-to-true would work; written out because the subset
      // refuses `readonly: false` and an author reading this file should see the
      // rule obeyed rather than defaulted.
      "readonly": true
    }
  ]

  // `host_devices` is deliberately ABSENT, and the omission is the R5 rule rather
  // than a shrug: the bundled `audio` loophole already passes `/dev/snd` through,
  // a second identical `--device /dev/snd` is UNMEASURABLE on the mandated
  // verification loop (RuntimeArgsFor skips device passthrough whenever the
  // launcher is itself in a jail — "devices cannot nest under rootless podman"),
  // and shipping a duplicate whose failure mode can only be observed on a
  // maintainer's real desktop is exactly the regression this pack must not be.
  // The device CLAIM class is pinned by test over an audio-shaped manifest
  // instead, which proves the enumeration without betting a jail on it.
}
